{
  "schemaVersion": "1.3",
  "kind": "archsim.architecture",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "Notifications: one campaign, three providers' quotas",
    "description": "A notification service that the app's other services call for order updates, security alerts and marketing campaigns. They POST each notification, with an idempotency key so their retries are safe, to a Go notification API behind an internal load balancer. The API answers once it has queued one message per channel, each channel on its own SQS queue: every notification goes out as a push and an e-mail, and the quarter whose recipients opted in to SMS (the write request type here; reads are the rest) also as a text. Each channel has its own sender deployment, sized to stay under its provider's quota: 32 push workers for a gateway that takes 1,000 calls a second, 10 e-mail workers (about 50 sends a second) for a 50/s quota, 12 SMS workers (about 26 a second) for 30/s. The base load is 12 notifications a second; at 20 s a marketing campaign adds about 3,100 in 8 s. Callers never wait for a provider: the quotas decide how late each channel is, which the queues' message age and dead letters show. All numbers are illustrative assumptions.",
    "tags": [
      "notifications",
      "sqs",
      "fan-out",
      "rate-limits",
      "third-party",
      "dead-letters",
      "backoff"
    ]
  },
